WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.004
[Music]

00:00:04.391 --> 00:00:04.891
Let's see...

00:00:04.891 --> 00:00:10.431
What's&nbsp;the next big thing after large language models?
The technology of large language models--which

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.560
is essentially, we could take a piece&nbsp;
of text, blank out some amount of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.840
and then say what will the next word be, try and&nbsp;
predict it, learn if you get it wrong and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:27.400
just repeat over billions of times.
You can take a picture and blank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.040
out bits of it and try to predict&nbsp;
what's in the part that's missing.&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.120
You can take a gene sequence and blank&nbsp;
out a piece of it and try and predict it.&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.760
So, this is then a very successful strategy&nbsp;
that can be used for all sorts of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.560
domains of human experience and science.
So we're going to see a lot of broader use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.680
of foundation models in the soon nearby years.
Even though large language models have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:01:00.280
incredibly successful they don't really seem&nbsp;
to build the kind of world models that human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:07.400
beings have where they have a sense of stuff in&nbsp;
a physical world and particular entities, human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:14.000
beings and their relations that's more abstracted&nbsp;
away from particular sequences of words.&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.720
And so that suggests we need some kind of&nbsp;
different model of artificial intelligence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.360
But I think it's fair to say no one really knows&nbsp;
the answer of how to do that successfully and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:31.440
that's why we're still doing academic research.
AI system asks what are some of the most promising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:39.080
and exciting real world applications of NLP&nbsp;
that you foresee emerging in the next few years?&nbsp;

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:46.720
Uh, tons! So I think we're going to see a lot&nbsp;
more NLP systems being deployed very successfully&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:53.960
everywhere. So a lot of websites already have&nbsp;
a little AI assistant in the bottom corner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:58.920
I think we're going to see a new generation&nbsp;
of ones powered by large language models&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:04.760
which really work much more fluently.
I think we're going to see much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:12.880
use of conversational assistants. So, you're&nbsp;
thinking about how to word an email to your&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.040
boss, or you're not quite sure what&nbsp;
you should say to your boyfriend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.480
It turns out that you can&nbsp;
get advice, drafting help,&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:27.760
suggestions of what to say from a large language&nbsp;
model that are actually useful these days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.720
You know, I think there's just a whole world&nbsp;
of ways that we can use these models because&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:39.560
our world is so language based and that we're&nbsp;
all just sort of exploring what some of the&nbsp;possibilities are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.694 --> 00:02:50.760
In the future will AI be so smart it won't need human input?
I think the honest answer is no one knows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:58.680
There is a conceivable world at some point in the&nbsp;
distant future where an AI system can just learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:05.280
everything it needs needs from the world around it&nbsp;
and it doesn't really need us to tell it things.&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:11.520
But I think there's also a lot of very heated&nbsp;
hype at the moment. Some people think that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:18.040
2030 there'll be computers that are better&nbsp;
than human beings at almost all tasks.&nbsp;

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:25.160
I think a lot of those views are fairly&nbsp;
irrational exuberance where people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:30.040
been getting a little bit too excited by&nbsp;
the very genuine big advances we made.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.560
But there are other ways in which humans'&nbsp;
knowledge and ability to operate in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:42.160
world is likely to be better than our AI&nbsp;
models for a number of decades to come.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.880
Will conversational assistants and&nbsp;
chat bots replace search engines?&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.240
I think there are different answers for&nbsp;
different kinds of searchers. So one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:57.880
case is that people just have a particular&nbsp;
question in mind like "when is the primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:04.040
going to be held in the election for California."
To the extent that I've got a question like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:09.800
yeah absolutely, conversational agents&nbsp;
LLMs are great for that sort of purpose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.240
then they're faster and better&nbsp;
than something else you could use.&nbsp;

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:17.480
You can also use them for all kinds of&nbsp;
other question-answering related purposes.&nbsp;

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.880
Sometimes people use search when they want&nbsp;
to take a deep dive themselves, right.&nbsp;

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:26.360
You know, they're buying a new tent and&nbsp;
they'd like to make up their own mind.&nbsp;

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.920
Then there's navigational queries&nbsp;
where they just want to be taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.200
to the page and that's obviously not&nbsp;
going to be covered by using a large&nbsp;language model.&nbsp;

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:39.880
[Music]

